From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 08:08:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08045/11362] Phase 3 refused: the 12-cell banked-levels grid
 cannot reach significance, and 36 paired inductions already say so

The pre-flight gate on Phase 3 asks whether the wired change perturbs >= 6 of
12 cells. It was NOT run live, because the grid it screens is refusable on
arithmetic grounded in Phase 1's 36 paired live inductions -- and the
pre-flight's own doctrine is that every cell after the verdict is settled is
pure cost.

The bound needs no guess about mechanism. Banked levels is DOWNSTREAM of the
induced engine, so a cell whose two arms produce the same induce outcome cannot
differ on banked levels: P(discordant endpoint) <= P(discordant induce), which
Phase 1 measured directly on the real induce prompts of all six games.

  required one-way discordant pairs for p<0.05 : 6 of 12

  channel A, tracks strict quality   P(disc)=5/36   E[one-way]=1.0   P(reach 6)=0.000   needs 68 cells
  channel B, tracks mere usability   P(disc)=17/36  E[one-way]=4.3   P(reach 6)=0.238   needs 16 cells

Channel B is the UPPER bound (usable-discordance is the most permissive notion
of "the arms differed at induce"), so:

  P(the 12-cell grid reaches p<0.05) <= 0.24

Three runs in four would return an uninterpretable null EVEN IF the treatment
works exactly as measured -- the same shape as the three nulls that paid for
this pre-flight.

Channel A is the likelier of the two: the defect gate raises the supply of VALID
engines a lot (13/36 -> 22/36) and CORRECT ones not at all (3 vs 2, p = 1.000),
and the trust gate that admits on correctness is untouched. Which channel is
real is what a live pre-flight would settle, and it is worth settling because it
picks 16 cells over 68.

No scored or online ARC game was played. Nothing was submitted.
